<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Adaptive Nucleus Truncation for Long-Form Reasoning</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ousmane Amadou Dia
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ousamdia@gmail.com</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Sampling plays an important role in long-form language-model reasoning. Over thousands of decoding steps, small changes in the candidate token set can compound into different reasoning trajectories, stability profiles, and final answers. Existing truncation methods such as top-<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, min-<math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, and fixed top-<math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> sampling improve over unrestricted sampling, but they rely on fixed thresholds that cannot adapt to changes in entropy, task difficulty, training stage, or generation budget. We introduce Adaptive Nucleus Truncation Sampling (ANTS), which extends top-<math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> sampling from a fixed decoding rule into an adaptive rollout-control mechanism for long-form generation. ANTS selects standardized neighborhoods around the maximum logit before temperature scaling, adapts the truncation width using an entropy-conditioned controller, and retains a no-truncation fallback arm to stabilize training when truncation becomes unsafe. On a 33B-total / 4B-active sparse Mixture-of-Experts reasoning model, ANTS improves average performance over percentage-based benchmarks by +1.9, +3.8, and +5.2 points at 8K, 16K, and 32K generation budgets, respectively. The strongest gains appear on instruction following and mathematical reasoning, with IFBench improving by more than 10 points at 32K and AIME 2025 improving by 7 points. Code generation reveals an important budget interaction. On Codeforces, ANTS trails the baseline at 8K, but reverses this gap and substantially improves ELO at 16K and 32K. These results suggest that sampler design should be treated not just as a decoding hyperparameter, but as part of how we stabilize and scale long-budget reasoning.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly evaluated in regimes where the generation budget is large enough to express multi-step reasoning, self-refinement, tool plans, and executable code <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madaan et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. In these settings, decoding is not a minor post-processing detail. The sampler controls which reasoning paths are reachable, how much stochasticity is exposed to the policy during RL rollouts, and how often a long response drifts into noisy, irrelevant, or unstable continuations. Classical decoding heuristics such as top-<math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, nucleus sampling, and min-<math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> attempt to balance quality and diversity by restricting the candidate token set before sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Holtzman et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. However, probability-thresholding methods such as top-<math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and min-<math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> operate after softmax, and therefore inherit the distortions introduced by probability normalization and temperature scaling. As a result, the same underlying logit geometry may produce substantially different candidate sets as temperature changes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang et al. (2025)</a></cite> recently argued that informative and noisy tokens are more cleanly separated in pre-softmax logit space than in probability space. Their top-<math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> sampler keeps tokens whose logits fall within a standard-deviation window of the maximum logit, yielding a candidate set that is invariant to positive logit rescaling. This insight provides the geometry we need, but not the policy for how aggressively to use it. A single fixed <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> cannot simply serve all token positions, tasks, checkpoints, and generation budgets. Some contexts are decisive and should be pruned sharply. Others are genuinely ambiguous and require a wider support. In unstable RL phases, no truncation may be preferable at all.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we turn top-<math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> from a fixed decoding heuristic into a budget-aware rollout controller. We call the resulting method <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Adaptive Nucleus Truncation Sampling</em> (ANTS). Under ANTS, sampling can be decomposed into three pieces: (i) logit-space support estimation, (ii) context-dependent thresholding, and (iii) stability control. First, ANTS selects the top-logit neighborhood as follows:</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{t}(n)=\Big\{v\in\mathcal{V}\,\Big|\,\ell_{t,v}&gt;\max_{u\in\mathcal{V}}\ell_{t,u}-n\cdot\sigma(\ell_{t})\Big\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" mathsize="1.600em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{t}(n)=\Big\{v\in\mathcal{V}\,\Big|\,\ell_{t,v}&gt;\max_{u\in\mathcal{V}}\ell_{t,u}-n\cdot\sigma(\ell_{t})\Big\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the pre-temperature logit vector and <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\ell_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\ell_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is its vocabulary-wise (<math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>) standard deviation. Second, it makes the width of this neighborhood entropy-conditioned, <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{t}=n_{0}+\gamma\cdot\mathcal{H}(p_{t}^{(0)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{t}=n_{0}+\gamma\cdot\mathcal{H}(p_{t}^{(0)})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}^{(0)}=\texttt{softmax}(\ell_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}^{(0)}=\texttt{softmax}(\ell_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is the unit-temperature distribution. Finally, it learns the truncation strength online with a Thompson-sampling bandit over finite <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> values and one explicit fallback arm <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K+1</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{K+1}=+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{K+1}=+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Selecting the fallback sets <math id="S1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{t}=\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{t}=\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, exactly recovering the baseline sampler.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The fallback arm is central to our method. Without it, training can start well under truncation but later overshoot in entropy, KL, gradient norm, log-ratio statistics, and related diagnostics because every rollout remains subject to some finite pruning pressure. The fallback, <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{K+1}=+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{K+1}=+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, gives the controller a learned way to stop truncating before these metrics become unstable. Thus, unlike fixed top-<math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, ANTS does not assume that truncation is always beneficial for decoding. In fact, it learns when truncation helps and when the safest action is to return to the untruncated rollout distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our main empirical finding is that ANTS improves with generation budget. This effect is clearest on IFBench and AIME 2025, where longer generations create more opportunities for verbose, irrelevant, or unstable continuations to accumulate. ANTS appears to preserve enough diversity for reasoning while suppressing tails that lead to unnecessary continuation. Codeforces provides the main caveat at 8K. The issue is not simply that truncation shortens completions. In fact, ANTS generates more solution tokens in this regime, yet trails the baseline in ELO. A more plausible explanation is that, under a tight budget, the induced distribution is less favorable for reaching correct complete programs. At 16K and 32K generation lengths, where the feasible program space is less constrained, the effect reverses into large ELO gains. This suggests that truncation should be interpreted jointly with generation budget, task type, and training state rather than as a fixed decoding hyperparameter.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contributions.</span> We formulate ANTS as an adaptive logit-space sampler with temperature-invariant support selection for RL rollouts. First, we introduce an entropy-driven Thompson controller that adapts truncation strength online and retains a no-truncation fallback arm. Second, we provide a generation-budget scaling evaluation across 8K, 16K, and 32K budgets, showing that gains generally grow with the generation budget and are especially strong for instruction following. Third, we characterize a short-budget code-generation caveat that motivates budget-aware truncation policies.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background and Motivation</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Probability-space truncation.</span> Nucleus sampling selects the smallest prefix of the probability-sorted vocabulary whose cumulative mass exceeds a threshold <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Holtzman et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. Min-<math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> instead uses the top token’s probability as a confidence-scaled threshold <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. While these methods are simple and effective, they both operate after softmax. Consequently, their candidate sets depend on the temperature-transformed distribution <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{softmax}(\ell_{t}/\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{softmax}(\ell_{t}/\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>. For long generations, small differences in candidate-set construction can compound over many steps and destabilize training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Logit-space truncation.</span> The top-<math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> sampling algorithm, introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, replaces probability thresholds with standardized distances in logit space. This is useful because the membership of Equation <a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Adaptive Nucleus Truncation for Long-Form Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is invariant to positive rescaling of logits. More formally, for any <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\ell_{t,v}&gt;\max_{u}\ell_{t,u}-n\sigma(\ell_{t})\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\omega\ell_{t,v}&gt;\max_{u}\omega\ell_{t,u}-n\sigma(\omega\ell_{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>u</mi></munder><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mi>ω</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>u</mi></munder><mi>ω</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{t,v}&gt;\max_{u}\ell_{t,u}-n\sigma(\ell_{t})\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\omega\ell_{t,v}&gt;\max_{u}\omega\ell_{t,u}-n\sigma(\omega\ell_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Temperature scaling corresponds to setting <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega=\nicefrac{{1}}{{\tau}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded voffset="0.3em"><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mpadded><mpadded style="width:-0.15em;" lspace="-0.1em" width="-0.15em"><mo stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo></mpadded><mi mathsize="0.700em">τ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega=\nicefrac{{1}}{{\tau}}</annotation></semantics></math> before softmax. Thus, if the nucleus set <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is selected before the logits are scaled by the temperature, the set of candidate tokens is temperature-invariant. More intuitively, the temperature only reshapes the probabilities inside the selected set.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Entropy conditioning.</span> A fixed top-<math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> threshold is insufficient across token positions. Some contexts are decisive, with one or a few plausible continuations. Others may, however, be genuinely ambiguous. Thus, the same fixed <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> cannot simultaneously serve mathematical derivations, instruction-following constraints, and code completions under different generation budgets. ANTS therefore adapts <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with a base entropy signal. Since Equation <a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Adaptive Nucleus Truncation for Long-Form Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> keeps tokens within <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> standard deviations of the maximum logit, increasing <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> makes the filter less aggressive. High-entropy contexts therefore retain more alternatives, while low-entropy contexts are pruned more sharply. This turns top-<math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from a fixed decoding heuristic into a context-dependent support estimator, motivating the entropy conditioning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Online controller.</span> The optimal truncation strength depends on task, generation budget, and training checkpoint. Rather than hard-coding a single <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, we cast truncation selection as a Thompson-style controller over a finite arm set. Because the candidate distributions for all arms can be evaluated on the same sampled logits, each arm receives an intrinsic entropy-based reward, while Thompson sampling is used for arm selection. Thompson sampling provides a simple exploration-exploitation mechanism with negligible overhead <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thompson, 1933</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agrawal and Goyal, 2012</a>)</cite>. The fallback arm is the <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K+1</annotation></semantics></math>-th arm with <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{K+1}=+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{K+1}=+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Operationally, it bypasses truncation and recovers the baseline sampler. This arm is important for stability in RL rollouts. Without it, training can begin with strong early gains but later overshoot in entropy, KL, gradient norm, log-ratio statistics, and related diagnostics because every rollout remains subject to some nonzero pruning pressure. The fallback arm gives the online controller a safe escape hatch when truncation is no longer beneficial.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{N})</annotation></semantics></math> be a prompt and <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{&lt;t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{&lt;t}</annotation></semantics></math> the generated sequence conditioned upon <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. At step <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, an LLM produces logits <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{V}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{V}|}</annotation></semantics></math>. ANTS first computes a logit-space nucleus, <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, masks logits outside the nucleus, and samples from the temperature-scaled distribution restricted to the surviving tokens:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{gathered}p_{t}^{\mathrm{ANTS}}(v)=\frac{\mathbbm{1}\{v\in\mathcal{N}_{t}\}\exp(\ell_{t,v}/\tau)}{\sum_{u\in\mathcal{N}_{t}}\exp(\ell_{t,u}/\tau)}.\end{gathered}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ANTS</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">}</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{gathered}p_{t}^{\mathrm{ANTS}}(v)=\frac{\mathbbm{1}\{v\in\mathcal{N}_{t}\}\exp(\ell_{t,v}/\tau)}{\sum_{u\in\mathcal{N}_{t}}\exp(\ell_{t,u}/\tau)}.\end{gathered}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This design separates candidate-set selection from within-set stochasticity. That is, <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by pre-temperature logits, while the scalar <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> still controls randomness among retained candidates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Entropy-conditioned logit-space nucleus.</span> For a selected arm <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(a_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(a_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> be its truncation parameter. This parameter defines the entropy-conditioned width <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> of the logit-space nucleus. Formally, we set</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{gathered}n_{t}=n_{0}+\gamma(a_{t})\cdot\mathcal{H}(p_{t}^{(0)}),\qquad p_{t}^{(0)}=\texttt{softmax}(\ell_{t}),\quad\text{where }\mathcal{H}(p)=-\sum_{v}p_{v}\log p_{v}.\end{gathered}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>n</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="4.167em">,</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">softmax</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mtext>where </mtext><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>v</mi></msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>v</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{gathered}n_{t}=n_{0}+\gamma(a_{t})\cdot\mathcal{H}(p_{t}^{(0)}),\qquad p_{t}^{(0)}=\texttt{softmax}(\ell_{t}),\quad\text{where }\mathcal{H}(p)=-\sum_{v}p_{v}\log p_{v}.\end{gathered}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The nucleus is then Equation <a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Adaptive Nucleus Truncation for Long-Form Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> with <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=n_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=n_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. In practice <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is clipped to a valid range <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n_{\min},n_{\max}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n_{\min},n_{\max}]</annotation></semantics></math> to prevent degenerate candidate sets. If the fallback arm is selected, we set <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{t}=\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{t}=\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, the vocabulary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Entropy is reused as both a local width signal and a controller reward. The unit-temperature entropy <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(p_{t}^{(0)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(p_{t}^{(0)})</annotation></semantics></math> determines the selected arm’s token-level nucleus width in Equation <a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3 Method ‣ Adaptive Nucleus Truncation for Long-Form Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, while the entropy of each arm-induced predictive distribution provides the intrinsic reward used for the bandit controller.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bandit controller.</span> We use a log-spaced grid of <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> truncation arms,</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\gamma_{k}=10^{\eta_{k}},\qquad\eta_{k}=\log_{10}\gamma_{\min}+\frac{k-1}{K-1}\big(\log_{10}\gamma_{\max}-\log_{10}\gamma_{\min}\big),\qquad\forall\,k=1,\cdots,K," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.337em">∀</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{k}=10^{\eta_{k}},\qquad\eta_{k}=\log_{10}\gamma_{\min}+\frac{k-1}{K-1}\big(\log_{10}\gamma_{\max}-\log_{10}\gamma_{\min}\big),\qquad\forall\,k=1,\cdots,K,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">plus a fallback arm <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K+1</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{K+1}=+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{K+1}=+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}=\{1,\cdots,K+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=\{1,\cdots,K+1\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of all <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K+1</annotation></semantics></math> arms, including the fallback arm. For bounded rewards <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, each arm maintains a Beta posterior <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha_{k},\beta_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha_{k},\beta_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>. At each sequence generation, the controller samples <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{q}_{k}\sim\texttt{Beta}(\alpha_{k},\beta_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">Beta</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{q}_{k}\sim\texttt{Beta}(\alpha_{k},\beta_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> and selects the arm <math id="S3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\arg\max_{k}\tilde{q}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\arg\max_{k}\tilde{q}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Because the nucleus sets for all arms can be constructed from the same sampled logits, we update every arm posterior at each step rather than observing feedback only for the selected arm. In principle, the reward for each arm can come from any bounded signal, including a task metric, verifier score, rule-based correctness signal, normalized judge score, or perplexity-based score. In our implementation, we use a lightweight intrinsic signal based on the entropy of the distribution induced by each arm. For each arm <math id="S3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute the entropy <math id="S3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{a,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{a,t}</annotation></semantics></math> over valid sampled decoding positions. We standardize these values within the current batch,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{gathered}\mathcal{S}_{h}=\Big\{h_{b,s},b\in\mathcal{A}\,\Big|\,m_{b,s}=1\Big\},\\
z_{a,t}=\frac{h_{a,t}-\mu_{h}}{\sigma_{h}+\epsilon},\qquad\mu_{h}=\texttt{mean}(\mathcal{S}_{h}),\qquad\sigma_{h}=\texttt{std}(\mathcal{S}_{h}).\end{gathered}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.340em" maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.507em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>m</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="4.167em">,</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">mean</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="4.167em">,</mo><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">std</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{gathered}\mathcal{S}_{h}=\Big\{h_{b,s},b\in\mathcal{A}\,\Big|\,m_{b,s}=1\Big\},\\
z_{a,t}=\frac{h_{a,t}-\mu_{h}}{\sigma_{h}+\epsilon},\qquad\mu_{h}=\texttt{mean}(\mathcal{S}_{h}),\qquad\sigma_{h}=\texttt{std}(\mathcal{S}_{h}).\end{gathered}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{a,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{a,t}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates whether arm <math id="S3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> produced a valid nondegenerate nucleus at position <math id="S3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. We then average over valid positions and map the averaged score to a bounded Thompson-sampling reward:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{a}=\texttt{sigmoid}(\bar{z}_{a}),\qquad\bar{z}_{a}=\frac{\sum_{t}m_{a,t}z_{a,t}}{\sum_{t}m_{a,t}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">sigmoid</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{a}=\texttt{sigmoid}(\bar{z}_{a}),\qquad\bar{z}_{a}=\frac{\sum_{t}m_{a,t}z_{a,t}}{\sum_{t}m_{a,t}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.4" class="ltx_p">The posterior <math id="S3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha_{a},\beta_{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha_{a},\beta_{a})</annotation></semantics></math> of each arm <math id="S3.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is updated as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{a}\leftarrow 1+(1-\rho)(\alpha_{a}-1)+r_{a},\qquad\beta_{a}\leftarrow 1+(1-\rho)(\beta_{a}-1)+(1-r_{a})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{a}\leftarrow 1+(1-\rho)(\alpha_{a}-1)+r_{a},\qquad\beta_{a}\leftarrow 1+(1-\rho)(\beta_{a}-1)+(1-r_{a}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is a decay factor. Higher-than-average entropy therefore receives a larger posterior update, favoring arms that preserve sufficient support rather than arms that collapse the candidate set too aggressively. This reward is inexpensive because it is computed from the same arm-induced candidate distributions already constructed by the sampler, avoiding separate reward models, judges, or verifiers. When memory or inference overhead is not a constraint, however, the same update rule can instead use external task rewards or learned reward-model scores. The fallback posterior is updated in the same way as the finite truncation arms, allowing the controller to learn when no truncation is preferable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fallback as stability control.</span> The fallback arm <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{K+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{K+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is more than a conservative option for inference. In rollout training, a sampler that is helpful early can become harmful later as the policy changes. If every arm applies finite truncation, the controller has no way to remove pruning pressure when rollout statistics begin to drift. Empirically, the no-fallback variant can start with strong gains but later overshoot in entropy, KL divergence, gradient norm, importance sampling or log ratio statistics, and related diagnostics. Treating <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{K+1}=+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{K+1}=+\infty</annotation></semantics></math> as a normal Thompson-sampling arm gives the online controller a learned escape hatch. Fundamentally, the controller can choose the baseline sampler when truncation is unsafe and return to finite truncation when the reward signal supports it.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Adaptive Nucleus Truncation Sampling with Thompson Controller</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
            
            
          Initialize <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> finite truncation arms <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\gamma_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\gamma_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> and fallback arm <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K+1</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{K+1}=+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{K+1}=+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
            
            
          Initialize Beta posteriors <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha_{k},\beta_{k})=(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha_{k},\beta_{k})=(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> for all arms.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each prompt <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
            
            
            Sample <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{q}_{k}\sim\mathrm{Beta}(\alpha_{k},\beta_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Beta</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{q}_{k}\sim\mathrm{Beta}(\alpha_{k},\beta_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> and choose <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\arg\max_{k}\tilde{q}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\arg\max_{k}\tilde{q}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,\ldots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,\ldots,T</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
            
            
             Compute logits <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{t}=f_{\theta}(x,y_{&lt;t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{t}=f_{\theta}(x,y_{&lt;t})</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
            
            
             <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=K+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=K+1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
            
            
               <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{t}\leftarrow+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{t}\leftarrow+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{t}\leftarrow\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{t}\leftarrow\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> fallback/no truncation.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
            
            
             <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
            
            
               <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{t}\leftarrow n_{0}+\gamma(a)\cdot\mathcal{H}(\texttt{softmax}(\ell_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{t}\leftarrow n_{0}+\gamma(a)\cdot\mathcal{H}(\texttt{softmax}(\ell_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
            
            
               <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{t}\leftarrow\{v\in\mathcal{V}:\ell_{t,v}&gt;\max_{u}\ell_{t,u}-n_{t}\sigma(\ell_{t})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{t}\leftarrow\{v\in\mathcal{V}:\ell_{t,v}&gt;\max_{u}\ell_{t,u}-n_{t}\sigma(\ell_{t})\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
   
</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
            
            
             Sample <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}\sim\texttt{softmax}(\ell_{t}/\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}\sim\texttt{softmax}(\ell_{t}/\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> restricted to <math id="alg1.l12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
  
</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
            
            
            Compute <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{a,t},z_{a,t},\bar{z}_{a,t},r_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{a,t},z_{a,t},\bar{z}_{a,t},r_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="alg1.l13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
            
            
            <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{a}\leftarrow\alpha_{a}+r_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{a}\leftarrow\alpha_{a}+r_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="alg1.l14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{a}\leftarrow\beta_{a}+1-r_{a},\,\forall\,a\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">∀</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{a}\leftarrow\beta_{a}+1-r_{a},\,\forall\,a\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experimental Setup</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Models and regimes.</span> We evaluate ANTS on a 33B-total/4B-active sparse MoE reasoning architecture across 8K, 16K, and 32K generation budgets. For each budget, we compare ANTS against a baseline sampler that does not apply logit-space truncation. Curves are plotted over RL training checkpoints.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmarks.</span> We evaluate across tasks that expose different failure modes of long-budget generation: AIME 2024 and AIME 2025 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang and Math-AI, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang and Math-AI, 2025</a>)</cite> for mathematical reasoning, GPQA Diamond <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rein et al., 2023</a>)</cite> for scientific QA, MMLU Pro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> for knowledge retrieval and multiple-choice reasoning, Codeforces ELO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Quan et al., 2025</a>)</cite> for executable code generation, IFBench Loose Prompt and Strict Prompt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pyatkin et al., 2025</a>)</cite> for long-form instruction following, and IFEval Strict and Loose instruction scores for instruction compliance. AIME, GPQA, MMLU Pro, IFBench, and IFEval are reported in percentage points, while Codeforces is reported in ELO.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Primary hypothesis.</span> Our guiding hypothesis is generation-budget scaling. Fundamentally, if ANTS suppresses noisy tails without removing useful reasoning paths, its benefit should increase as the generation budget grows and the cumulative risk of off-manifold continuation increases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation.</span> ANTS is applied to rollout generation during RL training rather than only at evaluation time. The baseline sampler is temperature-scaled sampling with no logit-space truncation. The controller maintains <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> finite truncation arms on a log-spaced grid. Concretely, each arm stores a log-scale value <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{k}\in[\log_{10}\gamma_{\min},\log_{10}\gamma_{\max}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{k}\in[\log_{10}\gamma_{\min},\log_{10}\gamma_{\max}]</annotation></semantics></math>, and the entropy multiplier used by the sampler is <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{k}=10^{\eta_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{k}=10^{\eta_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We set <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}=0,\gamma_{\min}=5,\gamma_{\max}=32,K=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}=0,\gamma_{\min}=5,\gamma_{\max}=32,K=10</annotation></semantics></math>, and use the selected arm to form the entropy-conditioned width <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{t}=\gamma_{k}\mathcal{H}(p_{t}^{(0)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{t}=\gamma_{k}\mathcal{H}(p_{t}^{(0)})</annotation></semantics></math>. The finite-arm nucleus width is clipped to <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n_{\min},n_{\max}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n_{\min},n_{\max}]</annotation></semantics></math> to prevent degenerate candidate sets. We include a fallback arm <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K+1</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to <math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{K+1}=+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{K+1}=+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, which bypasses clipping and sets <math id="S4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{t}=\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{t}=\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, exactly recovering the no-truncation baseline over <math id="S4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The controller is updated using an intrinsic entropy-based reward rather than an external verifier or judge. For each arm, including the fallback arm, we compute the entropy of the arm-induced predictive distribution on sampled decoding positions. These entropy scores are standardized within the batch, averaged over valid positions, and passed through a sigmoid to obtain a bounded reward in <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, arms with higher-than-average entropy receive larger posterior updates, favoring truncation levels that preserve sufficient support rather than collapsing the candidate set too aggressively. This reward is inexpensive because it reuses the candidate distributions already constructed for the different arms; no separate reward model, verifier, or judge is required. Evaluations are logged every 80 training steps, and all curves and table entries reflect per-checkpoint averages over the observed training window.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We organize the evaluation around four questions. First, <em id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">does ANTS improve as the generation budget grows?</em> Second, <em id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">where does logit-space truncation have the most leverage?</em> Third, <em id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">where is the sampler mostly irrelevant?</em> Finally, <em id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">when can truncation be unsafe, and what does the fallback arm buy us during rollout training?</em> We provide answers to these questions in the following sections.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>ANTS improvement over the no-truncation baseline across generation budgets. Percentage metrics are absolute point differences. Codeforces is ELO difference. Positive deltas are bolded.</figcaption>
